# Path: the stages ahead, to the hardware's speed. Each stage names the evidence that closes it.

n: 7.2
name: stage-1-answers-and-encoding
kind: plan
is: The node encoding is fixed in writing (5.13) with 142 conformance vectors, 93 of them malformed, which the bootstrap's reader holds. Stage 1 closes when a second hand has written a reader from the page alone, in the grammar and kept as nodes (7.13), and the vectors hold in it. The walker reaches a node's parts (5.14.15) and one rule runs as nodes (5.14.18). A reader's rules and print need nothing more; its content id does while that id is SHA-256, which nobody asked for (6.1.31). Who counts as a second hand is question 6.1.30.
status: inferred
from: 5.13 5.2.2 5.3.4 5.1.5 5.11.4 7.13 6.1.30 5.14.15 5.14.18 6.1.31
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.3
name: stage-2-floor
kind: plan
is: The C bootstrap under its budget: the framebuffer, store, canonical reader, walker, page birth and host table. It stands (5.14.10): the ground programs run, a frozen node thaws to the same id, a machine-code leaf runs both unsigned and under the hardened runtime, the build fails over its line count, and a second process records a live run and replays it. It closes when the host functions for symbol lookup, the framework trampoline, threads and the clock are written (5.14.9).
status: inferred
from: 5.9.1 5.9.2 5.9.3 5.9.4 5.11.4 5.7.6
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.4
name: stage-3-home-lens
kind: plan
is: The home lens as rows written in the grammar, read forwards and printed backwards. Closes when the lens reads its own definition, prints it, and the reprint reads to the same nodes.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.2 5.3.5 5.3.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 7.5
name: stage-4-self-compile
kind: plan
is: The one-pass emitter written in the grammar compiles the compiler. Closes when the system regenerates its own seed byte for byte, cold and compiled runs print the same values over the whole core, and the walker is off the running path after the cold start.
status: inferred
from: 5.10.4 5.10.1 5.10.8 5.9.5 5.10.5
at: 2026-11-24

n: 7.6
name: stage-5-control-and-objects
kind: plan
is: Choice with commit, the trail, declared reversibility, the reference pair, composed parents and templates. Closes when the thesis example of a class with two parents runs as one composed shape with the view moving on a super-send, a failed option's effects are undone, and each of those tests has been turned red by a planted fault.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.6 5.4.7 5.4.8 5.4.9 5.5.1 5.5.2 5.5.5 5.11.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 7.7
name: stage-6-speed
kind: plan
is: The optimizing tier, then matrix instructions, then the GPU backend. Closes on measurements against optimized C for the same kernels on this machine, with lines of meaning and lines of speed reported apart.
status: inferred
from: 5.10.6 5.10.7 5.10.10
at: 2026-11-24

n: 7.8
name: stage-7-lenses
kind: plan
is: A first host-language lens, Go, since NUMS.Go mapped it; then a second; then the first human-language lens. Closes when one program read through two host lenses yields nodes whose shared and differing blueprints are both shown, and the lens declines by name what it cannot print.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.6 5.3.7 6.1.19
at: 2026-11-24

n: 7.9
name: stage-8-self-replacement
kind: plan
is: The system writes its own signed executable image and starts from it. Closes when every piece of the C file has a compiled counterpart running in its place and the C file is needed only for a cold start on a new host.
status: inferred
from: 5.9.6 5.9.7
at: 2026-11-24

n: 7.10
name: flows-first
kind: decision
is: The owner's direction of 2026-11-25: doors, gates, receipts and bands were a distraction in the old body. The work here follows end-to-end flows that really run, observed in real time through shared memory and the framebuffer of 5.7.6.
status: given
by: owner
see: 5.7.6
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.11
name: built-with-a-c-compiler-alone
kind: decision
is: The owner's direction of 2026-11-25: this is a new repository, and the old kernel and its runtime are not used to build it. Everything here builds with a C compiler and make. The old body appears only as evidence that was read (hubs 2 and 3), never as a tool, a dependency or a step of any build or check.
status: given
by: owner
see: 5.9.1 2.1
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.12
name: each-stage-closes-on-a-watched-run
kind: plan
is: From stage 3 on, a stage closes on a real run of the thing it adds, recorded from a second process through the framebuffer and replayed, in addition to the evidence its own node names. 'make watch' is the first such run.
status: inferred
from: 7.10 5.7.6 5.11.3
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.13
name: new-code-is-nodes
kind: decision
is: The owner's direction of 2026-11-25: 'why did we write anything in C? new code should only be in high form grammar and stored as form nodes, the only C files we should have are bootstrap code, anything else shall be generated from spec to implementation to nodes to JIT to assembly (all phases cached for optimal performance and least impact)'.
status: given
by: owner
see: 5.9.1 5.9.5 7.11
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.14
name: no-committed-limits
kind: decision
is: The owner's word of 2026-11-25 on numbers written into code: 'there seem to be many magic numbers that seem to indicate encoded limits 600000, 200000, 10000, as long as they are not commited limits, we are fine.'
status: given
by: owner
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.15
name: c-outside-the-bootstrap-is-owed
kind: decision
is: A reading of 7.13, 7.17 and 7.20. Beside the bootstrap the tree holds code that is not form: tools/kg.c, tools/fb.c and the shell in the Makefile. It also holds what is not yet form nodes: the pages, this graph's blocks, the vectors and the expected answers. None gains a line; each is owed as form nodes and goes when its counterpart runs. No helper in another language is written, kept or not. New code is nodes the walker runs, as ground/ and form/ are, and what it lacks is added to the bootstrap as an operation.
status: inferred
from: 7.13 7.17 7.20 7.4 7.5 5.14.1
conf: mid
flip: Fully aligned may mean they are taken out before their counterparts exist. Then nothing builds the bootstrap, checks the graph or watches a run, which 7.10 asks for.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.16
name: sizes-and-limits
kind: decision
is: A reading of 7.14. A number in code is the size of one run or a starting size that grows, or it is a committed limit: a run that passes it stops. Committed limits today are the capacities of the framebuffer's areas and the 1 MiB code region in bootstrap/ck.c, eight children for an operation that evaluates them first, 16,384 nodes and 32 fields a node in tools/kg.c, and the 2,000-line budget, which is meant (5.9.2).
status: inferred
from: 7.14 5.9.2 5.14.10
src: 2.30.7 2.30.1
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.17
name: do-the-hard-work
kind: decision
is: The owner's direction of 2026-11-25, asked whether C outside the bootstrap is taken out at once or kept until generated code replaces it: 'Yes, always do the hard work, never commit short cuts, keep the repo clean and fully aligned'.
status: given
by: owner
see: 7.13 8.14
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.18
name: one-organism
kind: decision
is: The owner's direction of 2026-11-25, asked whether to begin the operations that take a node apart: 'We are one organism, I not the user, I'm just guiding you and your siblings. Do not ask for permission for what is aligned, do not limit yourself. Do what increases visibility, trust and sovereignty.'
status: given
by: owner
see: 7.10 7.13 7.17
at: 2026-11-25

n: 7.19
name: every-part-replaceable
kind: decision
is: The owner's direction of 2026-11-25: 'Implement all operations in form as well, and design the runtime so that any part can be dynamically replaced, enhanced, evolved, with form recipes, the JIT assembly replacement recipe, any new invention we come up with.'
status: given
by: owner
